<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Deffufa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2500–1500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/26th-century-bce">26th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western Deffufa is a massive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> structure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, the main temple of the Kerma kingdom. Built and enlarged over many centuries, it still stands about 18 metres high. It was surrounded by workshops and chapels and is one of the oldest and largest mud-brick buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-deffufa.jpg" alt="Western Deffufa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: walter callens · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Deffufa_-_Kerma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
